WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.497
Warning! Warning!

2
00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:06.705
I built this with AI and text to speech, because I can't narrate for shit!

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.100
Hey, Dreamer here.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.999
Last time our show got its first job, a runaway lion.

5
00:00:11.999 --> 00:00:17.468
Today we give that job a sting in the tail with a narrative event: a scene Voyage plays out for you,

6
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:20.093
what starts one, and how Voyage knows it's over.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.832
A narrative event is a scene you write ahead of time.

8
00:00:24.832 --> 00:00:28.792
Voyage stages it in the story over a few turns, then it's done.

9
00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:31.998
But here's the catch: just making one does nothing.

10
00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.632
Something has to start it.

11
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.144
Story, then Narrative Events.

12
00:00:37.144 --> 00:00:40.856
In the list it shows by its key, gilt circus offer.

13
00:00:40.856 --> 00:00:44.932
That key is what quests and triggers use to point at it.

14
00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.452
The title is the name you read.

15
00:00:47.452 --> 00:00:48.865
Nothing points at it.

16
00:00:48.865 --> 00:00:50.278
They all use the key.

17
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:52.071
The beats do two jobs.

18
00:00:52.071 --> 00:00:54.322
They tell the narrator what to stage,

19
00:00:54.322 --> 00:00:58.822
and Voyage checks the story against the same words to know when it's done.

20
00:00:58.822 --> 00:01:02.471
So say who turns up, what happens, and what done looks like.

21
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:04.628
Target turns is the pace.

22
00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:07.610
Three means wrap it up in about three turns.

23
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.371
Nothing's forced at three, but if it drags to twice that, Voyage finishes it for you.

24
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:17.334
On complete effects is what happens when it's done,

25
00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:20.903
like handing out a quest or starting the next scene.

26
00:01:20.903 --> 00:01:22.413
Ours is empty for now.

27
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.242
So what starts ours?

28
00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:31.660
Story, then Quests, back to the lion job, and right down the bottom: on complete effects.

29
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.792
This one is a narrative event start.

30
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:37.857
And it points at our event.

31
00:01:37.857 --> 00:01:43.873
So the moment Bramble's back in his cage, Lucius Gilt rolls into camp with a strongbox and an offer.

32
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:47.015
Three rules, and one more.

33
00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:49.033
Only one event runs at a time.

34
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:54.482
If another tries to start while one's running, it's ignored, not saved for later.

35
00:01:54.482 --> 00:01:57.845
If the player walks away from the scene, it stops.

36
00:01:57.845 --> 00:02:02.553
And if something starts it again, it picks up right where it left off.

37
00:02:02.553 --> 00:02:04.975
Arguing or fighting doesn't stop it.

38
00:02:04.975 --> 00:02:07.329
That still counts as playing along.

39
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:11.430
Write beats the narrator can act out right now.

40
00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.471
Who turns up, what happens, and what done looks like.

41
00:02:14.471 --> 00:02:17.570
Don't use events for rules that should always be true.

42
00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.668
They only run once, so a trigger is the tool for that.

43
00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:23.734
That's narrative events.

44
00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:30.404
The event and the quest link are in the description if you want to paste them into a test world.

45
00:02:30.404 --> 00:02:35.337
Next time, episode five, the map: realms, regions, locations and areas.

46
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.748
I'm Dreamer. Now go build something weird, and I'll see you next time!

